question:
We have an entity whose primary key comes from a sequence:

@Id
@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.SEQUENCE, generator = "id_key_gen")
@SequenceGenerator(name = "id_key_gen", sequenceName = "id_key_seq")
@Column(name = "id", unique = true, nullable = false)
public int getId()

On PostgreSQL the column is a serial. Running `select last_value from id_key_seq;` returns 1603. When the app persists a new instance, the Hibernate debug log shows:

SequenceStructure [DEBUG] - Sequence value obtained: 1604
AbstractSaveEventListener [DEBUG] - generated identifier: 1554, using strategy: org.hibernate.id.enhanced.SequenceStyleGenerator

The INSERT that follows uses 1554, not 1604. Hibernate clearly reads the right value from the sequence but then assigns a different ID, and we can't tell where 1554 comes from. This looks like a Hibernate bug. Someone suggested switching to GenerationType.AUTO, but nobody explained why that would help.

What is the most likely root cause, and how would you fix it?

We're building a simple chat room on Laravel with laravel-echo-server (socket.io), Redis as the queue/broadcast backend, Laravel Echo and Vue. A message typed into an input is POSTed to /chat. The route fires a MessageSent event that implements ShouldBroadcast on the public channel 'test-chat-channel'.

Observed behaviour: the first message produces one console log of the event. The second message produces two, the third produces three, and the count keeps growing with every send. Everything also feels sluggish.

Checks so far:
- laravel-echo-server shows the client joining the channel only once.
- The queue worker processes exactly one broadcast job per message.

Relevant front-end code:
```
methods: {
  sendMessage: function () {
    this.$http.post('/chat', {message: this.message}).then((response) => {
      window.Echo.channel('test-chat-channel')
        .listen('MessageSent', (event) => { console.log(event.message); });
    }, (response) => {});
    this.message = '';
  }
}
```

Why does a single broadcast show up more times with each message sent?

What is the most likely root cause, and how would you fix it?

Our nginx reverse proxy runs as a Docker container on ECS and fronts many virtual hosts in a single /etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf. It suddenly stopped and will not come back up. The container log shows:

[emerg] 1#1: host not found in upstream "dev-example.io" in /etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf:988

The failing block is an ordinary proxy vhost:

server {
  listen 80;
  server_name test.com;
  location / {
    proxy_pass http://dev-exapmle.io:5016/;
    proxy_redirect off;
    proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
    proxy_connect_timeout 90;
    ...
  }
}

The file also has a default_server on port 80 serving static files from /usr/share/nginx/html, plus dozens of other proxied servers. The dev backend for this one vhost recently became unreachable. Since then the entire nginx process exits at startup, taking every other site down with it. We need nginx to keep running and serving the healthy vhosts even when one backend host is gone.

What is the most likely root cause, and how would you fix it?

We front two app instances on the same host with nginx's upstream module. nginx listens on port 89 and proxies to an upstream group:

```
upstream lb {
  server 127.0.0.1:8081;
  server 127.0.0.1:8082;
}
location / {
  proxy_pass http://lb;
  proxy_redirect off;
  proxy_set_header Host $host;
  proxy_set_header X-Real-IP $remote_addr;
  proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
}
```

Normal round-robin works fine. When we stop one of the two backends to test failover, nginx keeps sending traffic to it. About half of all client requests hang and eventually time out instead of being served by the healthy instance. We expected nginx to notice the dead server on its own and send everything to the surviving one. That does not happen.

What is the most likely root cause, and how would you fix it?

We run an nginx reverse proxy in AWS. It sits behind a public ELB and forwards traffic to several internal ELBs, one per backend service. There are six server blocks in sites-enabled. They are identical except for the listen port and the upstream, for example:

```
server {
  listen 3000;
  location / {
    proxy_pass http://internal-prod732r8-PrivateE-1GJ070M0745TT-348518554.eu-west-1.elb.amazonaws.com:3000;
    include /etc/nginx/proxy.conf;
  }
}
```

About once every 24 hours, one of these proxied services stops responding through nginx. The other five keep working normally. Which one fails varies.

Restarting nginx brings the broken one back immediately, and everything is fine until the next occurrence. We checked the following and found nothing relevant:
- nginx error.log
- the access log, which looks unremarkable
- syslog
- dmesg

We are not sure whether the proxy config is wrong or whether there are other logs we should be checking.

What is the most likely root cause, and how would you fix it?

New test box on a Fedora/RHEL-style distro running nginx (worker user `nginx`) and php-fpm from distro packages, with the pool config in /etc/php-fpm.d/www.conf. Every request for a .php file returns 404. If the same file is renamed to .html, nginx serves it fine.

The server block uses `root /var/www/html` at the server level, in `location /`, and in the PHP location. The PHP location is:
```
location ~ \.php$ {
  root /var/www/html;
  try_files $uri =404;
  fastcgi_split_path_info ^(.+\.php)(/.+)$;
  fastcgi_pass unix:/var/run/php5-fpm.sock;
  fastcgi_index index.php;
  fastcgi_param SCRIPT_FILENAME $document_root$fastcgi_script_name;
  include fastcgi_params;
}
```
The server also defines `error_page 404 /404.html` and `error_page 500 502 503 504 /50x.html`. Neither page exists under the docroot.

The php-fpm service is running, the pool listens on the unix socket /var/run/php5-fpm.sock, and that socket file exists.

Already tried:
- reinstalling both packages
- chmod 0777 on the PHP scripts
- turning on `fastcgi_intercept_errors`
- re-checking the root directives at every level
- re-checking SCRIPT_FILENAME

The result is still a 404 on PHP only.

What is the most likely root cause, and how would you fix it?

I set up a new local site on PHP5 with nginx and PHP-FPM. Static HTML pages load fine. Any .php request, whether index.php in the webroot or a test phpinfo.php, makes Chrome show its generic "Oops! This link appears to be broken." page. Nothing useful appears in the site's error log.

Site config:
```
server {
 listen 80;
 server_name im;
 access_log /var/www/website/access.log;
 error_log /var/www/website/error.log;
 location / {
  root /var/www/website;
  index index.html index.htm index.php;
 }
 location ~ \.php$ {
  fastcgi_pass 127.0.0.1:9000;
  fastcgi_index index.php;
  fastcgi_param SCRIPT_FILENAME /var/www/website$fastcgi_script_name;
  include /etc/nginx/fastcgi_params;
 }
}
```
Already tried: chown -R www-data:www-data on the whole site directory, chmod 777 on the PHP file, and restarting both nginx and php-fpm. None of it changed anything. What is wrong?

What is the most likely root cause, and how would you fix it?
